// The peppy framework that runs the robots, as one MCP surface: the calls
// that change the stack of nodes, not the world and not the motion of a
// robot. Its one target names a daemon interface, stack_copies:v1: the peppy
// daemon that started the server serves it, and no node implements it. The
// launcher scopes the target: the robot options a client may add, the
// description of each, the features of each with their descriptions, and
// the most robots of these options the stack holds. So the document names
// no robot model, no option and no feature. A robot added here is a copy of
// its option with the features it names, each part on its machine, as
// `peppy stack join OPTION:NAME@CORE_NODE` makes it with one
// `--with AXIS=OPTION@CORE_NODE` word for each feature, the axis and the
// option that the scope gives that feature; a simulated robot's
// robot_initializer ends its start only once the simulation stands it.
//
// The launchers hub serves this document through the `robot_control` option
// of its `simulation_mcp` launcher, on port 8903, beside the robots'
// endpoint on port 8900, which drives the robots added here. The title, the
// opening of the instructions and every description say that the calls go
// to the framework's stack.
{
  peppy_schema: "mcp_exposure/v1",
  manifest: { name: "framework_controls", tag: "v1" },

  server: {
    title: "Robot stack (peppy framework)",
    instructions: "This endpoint controls the peppy framework that runs the robots: the stack of nodes, not the world and not the motion of a robot. Its tools add robots to the stack and remove them: stack.list reports the robot options this stack offers, the machines of the stack and the robots it can remove, stack.join adds one and stack.remove removes one. A robot option can offer features: parts that a robot of that option holds only when it is added with them. A robot and each of its features run on a machine: a peppy daemon, named by its core node name. A feature runs on the robot's machine, with the robot, or on another machine. A robot added here runs the same software as a robot of the same option that the command line adds with the words of the same features. The robots' endpoint drives it under its name, and a simulated robot also stands in the simulated world. Change rule: the stack runs one change at a time, so a call is refused while another addition, removal or other change of the stack runs; call again when it has ended. Wait rule: a call that ends before its work is done, by a cancel or a lost connection, does not stop the work, and stack.recent_calls records that call as cancelled. While the work runs, the change rule refuses another addition or removal, and stack.list reports the work under change. stack.list lists an added robot under copies once its addition has succeeded.",
  },

  call_record: {
    tool: "stack.recent_calls",
    description: "List the last calls of this endpoint that could change the stack, newest first, up to 200: every mutating tool and every action, with started_at, the client that made the call (its declared name and version, or null), the tool, its arguments as sent (null when larger than 1 kB, with arguments_bytes), outcome (running, completed, failed, cancelled or refused), success, message and duration_ms. Read outcome and success together: a completed call with success false ran to its end and reports a failure; a call refused by the change rule, and a call of stack.join refused by a name in use, by the limit, by a feature that is not a feature of its option or by a feature named twice, have outcome failed; and arguments outside the schema give outcome refused. The record holds the calls of MCP clients on this endpoint only; a robot added to or removed from the stack with peppy on the command line is not in it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    keep: 200,
  },

  targets: {
    // The copies of the launch, served by the peppy daemon within the
    // launcher's scope.
    stack: {
      daemon: { name: "stack_copies", tag: "v1" },
      services: [
        {
          member: "list",
          tool: "stack.list",
          description: "Report what this stack can add and remove, and its machines. options lists each robot option the stack can add: option, the value stack.join takes; description, one line that says what a robot of that option is and how it stands; and features, the features a robot of that option can have, each with feature, the value stack.join takes, and description, one line that says what the feature adds; features is empty when the option has none. robot.list on the robots' endpoint reports the model of an added robot under identity. core_nodes lists the machines of the stack now: this stack's machine and each live peppy daemon of the same workspace or project, each once, in name order. stack.list reads them on every call, so core_nodes changes while the stack runs: a daemon that starts is in the next answer, and a daemon that stops is not. this_core_node is the machine of this stack. copies lists each robot of these options on the stack, which stack.remove can remove: name, the robot's name; option; core_node, the robot's machine; and features, the features of its option that the robot holds, in the order of the option's features, each with feature and core_node, the machine that runs it. A robot whose core_node, or the core_node of one of its features, is not in core_nodes has software on a machine that is not live now. A robot whose software stopped, for example one that a simulation took out, stays listed until stack.remove removes it. Each item of copies is a valid stack.join input: for a robot that stack.join added, it makes the same robot on the same machines; a robot that the command line added can differ from the robot that its item makes. max_copies is the most robots of these options on the stack: stack.join refuses when the stack holds that many. change is present only while the addition or the removal of a robot of these options runs on the stack, from this endpoint or from any other client: action, join or remove, name, the robot's name, and option. A robot that is being added is in copies only once its addition has succeeded. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
      actions: [
        {
          member: "join",
          tool: "stack.join",
          description: "Add a robot of option to the stack under name, with the features it names, and wait until its software runs on each of its machines. Every field is required. name is 1 to 63 letters, digits, '_' or '-', not self, and not the name of a robot on the stack. A simulation can refuse a name by a rule of its own: Waldo refuses two names where one, followed by '_', begins the other, so bravo_2 and bravo cannot stand together. option is one of the options stack.list reports. core_node is the robot's machine, one of the core_nodes stack.list reports, usually this_core_node. features lists the features to add, each with feature, one of the features stack.list reports for option, and core_node, the machine that runs it, one of core_nodes; features is empty for a robot with no feature. Progress messages report each step; building the robot's software on each of its machines, and fetching its files into a simulation, can take minutes the first time. After the call, with success true, the robot's software runs on the stack, on the robot's machine and on the machine of each feature, and robot.list on the robots' endpoint lists it; a simulated robot stands in the simulation, where the simulation puts a robot that names no spot, and scene.get_robots_list on the simulated world's endpoint lists it with attached true. With success false, the addition is undone, and message says why and names the log files; when message says that the copy remains listed, stack.remove removes it. Refused, before the stack changes, for a feature that is not a feature of option, for a feature named twice, when the stack holds max_copies robots of the listed options, when a robot on the stack has the name, and by the change rule. A machine that is not live ends the call with success false before the stack changes, and message lists the live machines. It does not stand a simulated robot at a work surface: workspace.stand_at on the simulated world's endpoint does, and workspace.describe there says whether the robot can work it. See the wait rule.",
          operation: "long_running",
          progress_timeout_ms: 660000,
        },
        {
          member: "remove",
          tool: "stack.remove",
          description: "Remove a robot from the stack, and wait until its software has stopped. name is one of the copies stack.list reports. Progress messages report each part of the robot's software that stops, on each machine that runs it. On a machine that is not live, nothing stops: a progress message warns about it, and that part of the robot's software stays there until the machine's stack is reset. After the call, with success true, the robot's software has stopped on every live machine of the stack, robot.list on the robots' endpoint no longer lists it, and a call in progress on it has ended with an error. A simulation then takes the robot out of its world: scene.get_robots_list stops listing it once the world is rebuilt without it, and until then the simulation holds the name, so stack.join under the same name can end with success false; call it again. Refused for a name stack.list does not report, and by the change rule. See the wait rule.",
          operation: "long_running",
          progress_timeout_ms: 660000,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
